1. Directory structure
terraform-project/
│
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── outputs.tf
├── providers.tf
│
└── modules/
    └── networking/
        ├── main.tf
        ├── variables.tf
        └── outputs.tf
2. Module: modules/networking/main.tf
resource "aws_vpc" "this" {
  cidr_block = var.vpc_cidr

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.project_name}-vpc"
  }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "this" {
  for_each = var.subnets

  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.this.id
  cidr_block        = each.value.cidr_block
  availability_zone = each.value.availability_zone

  tags = {
    Name = "${var.project_name}-${each.key}"
  }
}
3. Module variables

modules/networking/variables.tf

variable "project_name" {
  type = string
}

variable "vpc_cidr" {
  type = string
}

variable "subnets" {
  type = map(object({
    cidr_block        = string
    availability_zone = string
  }))
}
4. Module outputs

modules/networking/outputs.tf

output "vpc_id" {
  value = aws_vpc.this.id
}

output "subnet_ids" {
  value = {
    for key, subnet in aws_subnet.this :
    key => subnet.id
  }
}
5. Call the module

Now your root main.tf can use the module:

module "networking" {
  source = "./modules/networking"

  project_name = "gopal-project"

  vpc_cidr = "10.0.0.0/16"

  subnets = {
    app_subnet = {
      cidr_block        = "10.0.1.0/24"
      availability_zone = "us-east-1a"
    }

    db_subnet = {
      cidr_block        = "10.0.2.0/24"
      availability_zone = "us-east-1b"
    }
  }
}

Then:

terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply
Reuse the SAME module

This is where Terraform modules become powerful.

You can call the same module again:

module "networking_dev" {
  source = "./modules/networking"

  project_name = "gopal-dev"

  vpc_cidr = "10.10.0.0/16"

  subnets = {
    app = {
      cidr_block        = "10.10.1.0/24"
      availability_zone = "us-east-1a"
    }

    db = {
      cidr_block        = "10.10.2.0/24"
      availability_zone = "us-east-1b"
    }
  }
}

And again for production:

module "networking_prod" {
  source = "./modules/networking"

  project_name = "gopal-prod"

  vpc_cidr = "10.20.0.0/16"

  subnets = {
    app = {
      cidr_block        = "10.20.1.0/24"
      availability_zone = "us-east-1a"
    }

    db = {
      cidr_block        = "10.20.2.0/24"
      availability_zone = "us-east-1b"
    }
  }
}

The important point is:

                ┌─────────────────────┐
                │ networking module   │
                │                     │
                │ VPC                 │
                │ Subnets             │
                │ Route tables        │
                │ Internet Gateway    │
                └──────────┬──────────┘
                           │
             ┌─────────────┼──────────────┐
             │             │              │
             ▼             ▼              ▼
          DEV          STAGING           PROD
       10.10.0.0/16   10.15.0.0/16    10.20.0.0/16

The code remains the same. Only the input variables change.

Using a GitHub module

Once you upload your module to GitHub, you don't need:

source = "./modules/networking"

You can use:

module "networking" {
  source = "git::https://github.com/gopal1409/terraform-aws-modules.git//modules/networking?ref=v1.0.0"

  project_name = "gopal-project"

  vpc_cidr = "10.0.0.0/16"

  subnets = {
    app = {
      cidr_block        = "10.0.1.0/24"
      availability_zone = "us-east-1a"
    }

    db = {
      cidr_block        = "10.0.2.0/24"
      availability_zone = "us-east-1b"
    }
  }
}

Then:

terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply

Terraform downloads the module from GitHub.

Version/tag it

For example:

v1.0.0
v1.1.0
v2.0.0

Then:

source = "git::https://github.com/gopal1409/terraform-aws-modules.git//modules/networking?ref=v1.0.0"

This is particularly useful because your infrastructure code can consume a known version of the module rather than whatever happens to be on the GitHub main branch.
